     <actual value="3">
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">A differentiated service delivery approach was agreed upon for youth accessing ART services at Buyiga HCIII. This initiative included appointing a designated focal health worker to attend to the needs of youth rightsholders, ensuring they receive tailored support and guidance throughout their HIV treatment journey. Youth rightsholders on the island were also charged with identifying their peers in need of ART services but did not visit the health facility to access such services due to stigma. Rightsholders living with mental disability and epilepsy to identify their peers and raise awareness about the regular psychiatric clinic at Butoolo HCIII This would upscale demand and access to psychiatric services. The 2 above indicators were achieved by the establishment of a  peer support network by the Fisherfolk Ambassadors for PWDs in Kammengo and Fisherfolk Ambassador in Buyiga who is also a peer mentor at Buyiga HCIII. They encouraged peers (living with mental disability, epilepsy and youth living with HIV) to connect, share experiences, and identify others in similar situations, fostering a sense of community.
All rightsholders living with disabilities to have their details captured in the facility register when they visit the health facility; to continuously support the Facility In-Charge to request for drugs,  services or lobby for support as informed by rightsholder data in the facility register. This was achieved by the appreciation of the use of data in advocacy by both rightsholders and social actors.</narrative>
      </comment>
